	Motif is definitely NOT being frozen.  Work is underway on
	Motif 2.0 now.  (1.2 is the current version)  The work is
	called the GAIA initiative, which stands for Graphic Application
	Interchange Architecture.  It's intended to extend Motif to
	new functionality in the areas of graphics and perhaps multi-
	media.
>Who is going to develop them further? 

	Mostly OSF.  However, some of the development that OSF
	"does" is actually development by member/sponsor companies
	that OSF does the engineering and product management of.
	If you hear of OSF "stopping development" that's what you
	are hearing about.

>
>Has the contributing companies in some way promised to share their new 
>developments of the DCE technologies with OSF for future incorporation in the 
>OSF/DCE?

	That I couldnt' say about.  My GUESS is that the expectation is
	that future development of things like DCE will actually happen
	inside OSF or under OSF contracts.

>
>Can the contributing companies develop and market their own better products
>based on these technologies which isn't necessarily compatible with DCE?
>
	Probably but would it be a good idea?  They get royalties for
	what OSF sells.  It's in their interest to have the market be
	as big as possible.

>Who is responsible for interoperability between two platforms running
>OSF/DCE when you have your service agreement with two companies? Who
>can decide which company should be responsible for a certain problem?
>
	I think this is a little like "who is responsible for
	interoperability between two platforms running SUN ONC"?
	What if neither is a Sun?  I thin the answer is that you are.
	This open systems stuff carries that implication with it
	pretty clearly.

Tony


