Sure, but how about company X extending DCE to support X's whizbang system in
an optimised fashion. This is a proprietary extension which not neccessarily
finds its way back to the original DCE source.

>>Who is responsible for interoperability between two platforms running
>>OSF/DCE when you have your service agreement with two companies? Who
>>can decide which company should be responsible for a certain problem?
>>
>	I think this is a little like "who is responsible for
>	interoperability between two platforms running SUN ONC"?

This is a bit of a practical problem. If company X sells you a TCP/IP
implementation for your whizbang system, it's very easy to tell if it's good
or not. Just plug your whizbang to your LAN and use some TCP/IP applications
on it against say the Sun next door. If it works, fine. If not, give it back
to X. The same is true for something as widely available as ONC-RPC. The
problem gets tighter if you start using technology that is not widely
available. If X sells you ONC-RPC for your Sun, just keep using it against
any of your other Systems. You can't do that with DCE (today, the situation
might change) since there are only few installations around. So if X says
that its DCE port for whizbang is o.k and Y says the same for their port to
the foobar system and still both won't talk to each other, who do you blame?
You might end up either buying expensive DCE validation software (is that
available at all ?) or hiring expansive consultants to find your problem.


>	What if neither is a Sun?  I think the answer is that you are.
>	This open systems stuff carries that implication with it
>	pretty clearly.

This is very true. If customers use open systems in the real sense of the
word open (i.e combing DEC machines with Cisco Routers, managing all that
with a Sun) then it's pretty hard to find anyone to kick in the ass if
something doesn't work properly. As a rule, I would never ever as a customer
combine any mix of products on my own risk. I'd rather find some company to
integrate all that for me in order to kick them if things go wrong.


