> Sure, but how about company X extending DCE to support X's whizbang system in
> an optimised fashion. This is a proprietary extension which not neccessarily
> finds its way back to the original DCE source.

I imagine that different vendors will do different things with DCE, yet
retain compatibility.  Whether it makes its way back to ``the original
DCE source'' is another matter, and basically solely up to the OSF.  In
your example, company X may decide to offer whizbang support to the OSF
in exchange for something, if the OSF decides it's a good thing. 
Company X may offer whizbang support for free, if it thinks that it
would be a good idea for such support to be widespread.  (The OSF might
or might not decide to accept ``whizbang support,'' depending on how the
support is integrated into the rest of the system.)  Or, company X may
not want to distribute DCE whizbang support except with its own whizbang
system, but presumably in any case its whizbang support would
interoperate with non-extended DCE.  It probably depends on what company
X thinks will sell the most of its whizbang systems that won't
necessarily sell competing systems (existing or hypothetical) that do
pretty much the same thing as their whizbang system.

> >>Who is responsible for interoperability between two platforms running
> >>OSF/DCE when you have your service agreement with two companies? Who
> >>can decide which company should be responsible for a certain problem?
> >>
